WEBVTT

NOTE Synthetic transcript written by hand as a demo. It does not come from a real video.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Today we are going to look at how a convolution
actually works on an image.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:14.000
A kernel is a small grid of numbers, often three by three,
that slides across the picture one position at a time.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:22.000
At every position we multiply the kernel against the pixels
underneath it and add the results into a single number.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:29.500
That number becomes one pixel of the output, which we call
a feature map.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:39.000
Different kernels pick out different things. One might respond
to horizontal edges, another to vertical ones.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:47.000
Stride controls how far the kernel jumps between positions,
so a larger stride gives you a smaller output.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Padding adds a border of zeros so the kernel can sit on the
corners without running off the edge of the image.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Pooling then shrinks the feature map by keeping only the
strongest response in each little neighbourhood.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Stacking these operations is what lets a network go from raw
pixels to shapes and eventually to objects.
